



{"id":"930f91d5-1980-4875-87d8-0bbedeb830e7","object":"chat.completion","created":1786442312,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_032\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 19,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 0,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 3,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_03\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 0,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a complex integrated repair optimization task. The mission order is fixed per UAV, and UAV assignments are fixed. We need to produce a JSON response with decisions for each mission.\n\nLet's understand the problem and constraints.\n\nInstance:\n- Fleet: UAV_01 and UAV_02. Both start at VP_05.\n- UAV_01: initial_soc_kwh overridden to 1.793999 (from intervention). Battery capacity 16.0, reserve 1.5, charge per block 1.2 kWh. Compatible layers L120_220 and L220_320. Empty energy 0.1 kWh/km, payload energy 0.026 kWh/km per kg. Travel time coefficient 1.0.\n- UAV_02: initial_soc 4.925306, capacity 22.0, reserve 2.5, charge per block 0.95. Empty energy 0.145, payload energy 0.012. Travel time coefficient 0.86.\n\nMissions ordered by UAV:\n\nUAV_01 missions (sequence):\n- MISSION_05: sequence_index 0 (but wait, looking at baseline, MISSION_05 is assigned to UAV_02? Actually check: baseline shows MISSION_05 with uav_id UAV_02. But in public task, under missions, MISSION_05 has uav_id UAV_02. So assignment is fixed: UAV_02 missions: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_04 (seq 1), MISSION_01 (seq 2). UAV_01 missions: MISSION_02 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1). That matches baseline.\n\nLet's confirm: mission list:\n- MISSION_01: uav_id UAV_02, seq 2, release 19, due 23, origin VP_04, dest VP_03, payload 0.5, path options: PATH_01 (travel slots 1, distance 8.32, energy 1.25632, max wind 0, corridor COR_05).\n- MISSION_02: uav_id UAV_01, seq 0, release 0, due 6, origin VP_05, dest VP_02, payload 0.5, path options: PATH_04 (7 slots, 36.4 km, energy 4.565652, wind 1), PATH_07 (2 slots, 2.08 km, energy 0.23504, wind 0), PATH_09 (3 slots, 2.2 km, energy 0.2486, wind 0).\n- MISSION_03: uav_id UAV_01, seq 1, release 9, due 18, origin VP_02 (expected incoming origin VP_02, but that's the origin after MISSION_02), dest VP_01, payload 0.5, path: PATH_05 (1 slot, 0.832 km, energy 0.104358, wind 1).\n- MISSION_04: uav_id UAV_02, seq 1, release 3, due 20, origin VP_02, dest VP_04, payload 0.5, path options: PATH_02 (2 slots, 1.04 km, energy 0.15704, wind 0, corridor COR_07), PATH_06 (14 slots, 8.32 km, energy 1.337981, wind 1, corridor COR_01), PATH_08 (2 slots, 1.04 km, energy 0.167248, wind 1, corridor COR_08). Note: COR_08 is closed for many slots (from slot 3 to 42). COR_07 closed slots 3-14. COR_09 capacity override: capacity 1 at slot 0.\n- MISSION_05: uav_id UAV_02, seq 0, release 0, due 2, origin VP_05, dest VP_02, payload 0.5, path: PATH_03 (1 slot, 2.08 km, energy 0.31408, wind 0, corridor COR_09).\n\nDeadhead: all incoming deadheads null, so no deadhead flights before missions.\n\nTurnaround slots: 1 at each destination.\n\nCharger capacity: default 2 per vertiport, no overrides given (except intervention charger_capacity_overrides is empty list). So all vertiports have 2 chargers.\n\nCorridor capacities: default 2, with override: COR_09, slot 0, capacity 1. So at slot 0, only one flight can use COR_09. Since MISSION_05 (UAV_02) uses COR_09 and departs slot 0, that's okay. MISSION_02 uses PATH_07 (COR_09) at maybe slot 0? That would conflict because COR_09 capacity 1 at slot 0. So we must avoid having two flights on COR_09 at slot 0.\n\nClosures: COR_08 closed slots 3-42 (so unavailable). COR_07 closed slots 3-14. So PATH_08 cannot be used for MISSION_04 if departure falls in those slots. PATH_02 uses COR_07, so if MISSION_04 departs during slots 3-14, PATH_02 is unavailable. PATH_06 (COR_01) is long 14 slots, available.\n\nWind levels: corridors indicate wind levels per segment. UAV energy consumption computed using: total energy = empty_energy_per_km * distance + payload_energy_per_km_kg * distance * payload_kg, multiplied by (1 + wind_energy_multiplier_per_level * wind_level) if wind > 0? Actually, the given energy values in path options already account for UAV characteristics and wind? Look: for MISSION_02 PATH_04: distance 36.4, payload 0.5, wind 1. UAV_01 empty energy 0.1, payload 0.026. So raw energy = 0.1*36.4 + 0.026*36.4*0.5 = 3.64 + 0.4732 = 4.1132. With wind multiplier? UAV_01 wind_energy_multiplier_per_level 0.11. So with wind 1, factor = 1.11. 4.1132*1.11 = 4.565652, matches given. So path energies already include wind. Travel slots given, and travel time coefficients? travel_time_coefficient of UAV: for UAV_01, 1.0, so travel slots as given. For UAV_02, travel_time_coefficient 0.86 - does that affect travel slots? The path travel slots are specified per path and likely already account for UAV coefficient? In baseline, MISSION_01 (UAV_02) PATH_01 travel_slots 1, and it's used. That path's travel_time_min_unrounded is 0.043. UAV_02 coefficient 0.86, but slot duration is 5 minutes? The travel slots value is integer number of slots. Possibly the slots are computed from base travel time divided by slot duration, and UAV coefficient influences actual travel time. But the problem says travel_slots are given, so we use those numbers for duration. So travel slots are fixed for each path.\n\nNow, the optimization objectives: lexicographically: urgent holds (none are urgent? all priorities routine, so none), total holds (minimize number held), repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nRepair distance: likely means Hamming distance from the baseline decisions? The baseline gives specific decisions. We want minimal changes (repair) perhaps. The task says \"repair task\", and \"repair distance\" is an objective. So we should keep as many decisions as possible same as baseline, only changing what's necessary due to interventions. This is a zero-shot repair: given a baseline plan and interventions, produce a new plan that satisfies all constraints and minimizes changes from baseline in the lexicographic order.\n\nSpecifically: urgent holds (if any mission priority urgent and held), total holds (total number held), repair distance (number of decisions differing from baseline? maybe measure of diffs: if held status differs, path_id differs, depart_slot differs, charge_blocks differs; sum of absolute slot changes? definition unclear, but likely just minimize number of changed decisions or sum of absolute depart_slot differences? Typically in this competition, \"repair distance\" is the sum of absolute depart_slot differences plus some penalty for path changes. However, given lexicographic ordering: first urgent holds, then total holds, then repair distance. So we want to avoid holding missions if possible, but if unavoidable, hold as few as possible. Then minimize deviation from baseline.\n\nWe must also respect constraints: energy/SOC, reserve, charging, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, turnarounds, order of operations.\n\nGiven baseline: \nMISSION_01: held false, uav UAV_02, path PATH_01, depart_slot 19, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_02: held false, UAV_01, PATH_04, depart_slot 0, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_03: held false, UAV_01, PATH_05, depart_slot 9, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_04: held false, UAV_02, PATH_08, depart_slot 3, charge_blocks 0.\nMISSION_05: held false, UAV_02, PATH_03, depart_slot 0, charge_blocks 0.\n\nNow interventions:\n- initial_soc override for UAV_01: 1.793999 (was 7.17 in baseline? In fleet, baseline had 7.17001, now override 1.793999). So UAV_01 starts with very low battery.\n- closed corridors: COR_08 closed slots 3-42. So PATH_08 for MISSION_04 is unavailable if depart_slot 3 (baseline depart 3). Slot 3 starts at 3 and travels slots 2? PATH_08 travel_slots 2, so it occupies slots 3 and 4? Actually, edge_segments for PATH_08 show: duration_slots 2, offset_slot 0. So it uses corridor COR_08 for slots 3 (depart) and maybe 3+1? Typically, if travel_slots = 2, it occupies the corridor for 2 consecutive slots starting at depart_slot. Closed corridor slots include slot 3 and 4? The closure list has COR_08 closed slots 3,4,5,...,42. So depart_slot 3 would use slot 3 and 4, both closed, so invalid. So MISSION_04 cannot use PATH_08 with depart_slot 3. It could use PATH_08 with depart_slot 0,1,2? But check: departure slot must be >= release_slot 3. So earliest depart is 3. So PATH_08 is impossible because any departure >=3 hits closure (since it needs slots 3 and 4 if depart 3, or later slots all closed). So MISSION_04 must use a different path: either PATH_02 (COR_07, travel slots 2) or PATH_06 (COR_01, travel slots 14). But COR_07 closed slots 3-14. So if depart_slot between 3 and 13 (or 14? closure includes slot 3 to 14. Travel slots 2: corridors occupied slots depart_slot and depart_slot+1. If depart_slot=3, uses 3,4 both closed. depart_slot=4 uses 4,5 closed. So PATH_02 is also unavailable if depart_slot in 3..13. The only option is to depart after slot 14: earliest depart_slot 15? Check closures: COR_07 closed 3-14. So slot 15 onward open. Release slot is 3, due 20. So we could depart at 15, uses slots 15,16. Then arrival at 16? travel 2 slots: depart 15, arrive 15+2=17. Turnaround 1 slot, so available at 18. Could then do MISSION_01? Let's check sequence: UAV_02 missions: MISSION_05 (seq0), MISSION_04 (seq1), MISSION_01 (seq2). So after MISSION_05, we do MISSION_04, then MISSION_01. MISSION_05 baseline depart 0, travel 1 slot, arrives slot 1 (since travel from VP_05 to VP_02, path PATH_03 travel 1 slot). Then turnaround 1 slot, available from slot 2. Then MISSION_04 release 3, can depart at 3 or later. To avoid COR_07 closure, depart at 15 arriving 17, turnaround to 18. Then MISSION_01 release 19, due 23, travel 1 slot from VP_04 to VP_03. But wait: MISSION_04 destination is VP_04. So after MISSION_04, UAV is at VP_04. MISSION_01 origin is VP_04. So no deadhead needed. So MISSION_01 can depart at 19 (release) or later. Arrive 20. Due 23, okay. So that works if we postpone MISSION_04 to 15.\n\nBut also need energy. UAV_02 starts at VP_05 with 4.925306 kWh. MISSION_05: path PATH_03 energy 0.31408, travel 1 slot. Depart 0, arrive 1. Energy used: 0.31408. After arrival, SOC = 4.925306 - 0.31408 = 4.611226. Reserve check: reserve 2.5. 4.611226 >= 2.5, ok. Turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge. We can charge up to charge_blocks. charge_kwh_per_block 0.95. Max charge blocks 3. We need enough charge for later missions. MISSION_04: we plan to use PATH_02 (energy 0.15704) or PATH_06 (1.337981). If we use PATH_02, energy 0.15704. Then MISSION_01: PATH_01 energy 1.25632. Total needed after MISSION_05: MISSION_04 + MISSION_01 = 0.15704 + 1.25632 = 1.41336. Battery after MISSION_05 is 4.611226, so plenty, no charging needed to complete. But we must still respect charging process if we want. However, no requirement to charge beyond what's needed. We can set charge_blocks 0. But note: after turnaround, charge_blocks is an action. Even if we don't charge, it's fine. So energy wise, it's okay.\n\nBut there is also the corridor capacity for COR_09 at slot 0: capacity 1. Both MISSION_05 (PATH_03) and MISSION_02 could use COR_09. Baseline has MISSION_02 using PATH_04 (COR_04) depart 0, travel 7 slots, so that avoids COR_09 conflict. But now UAV_01 has low initial SOC: 1.793999. MISSION_02 baseline path PATH_04 energy 4.565652. Starting energy 1.793999 is insufficient. So MISSION_02 cannot use PATH_04 because it would deplete battery below reserve? Reserve for UAV_01 is 1.5. After 4.565652, SOC would be negative. So must use a shorter path. Options: PATH_07 (COR_09, energy 0.23504, travel 2 slots) or PATH_09 (COR_02, energy 0.2486, travel 3 slots). Both feasible energy-wise. But PATH_07 uses COR_09. If MISSION_05 already uses COR_09 at slot 0, and COR_09 capacity at slot 0 is 1, then MISSION_02 cannot depart at slot 0 using COR_09. Could MISSION_05 depart at slot 0? MISSION_05 release 0, due 2. PATH_03 travel 1, so must depart at 0 or 1? If depart 0, arrive 1, due 2, okay. If depart 1, arrive 2, due 2 (if allowed arrival at due slot? due slot means latest arrival? Typically due slot is deadline, arrival must be <= due slot. Arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. So for depart 1, travel 1, arrive 2, due 2, okay. But we want to minimize changes from baseline. Baseline had MISSION_05 depart 0. Could we shift it to depart 1? Then COR_09 slot 0 free for MISSION_02. But then MISSION_02 could use PATH_07 at slot 0, arrival slot 2. MISSION_05 depart 1, arrival 2. Both arrive slot 2, possible if pad capacity at VP_02? VP_02 pad capacity 2, so two simultaneous arrivals allowed. Also turnaround slots: both arrive VP_02 at slot 2, turnarounds occupy slots 2-2? Turnaround slot is 1 slot after arrival before available? The specification says: \"turnaround: 1 slot\". Typically, after service flight arrival, UAV is unavailable for that turnaround slot, then becomes available. The pad capacity might be affected during turnaround? But probably pad capacity applies to parking, not specifically to active turnaround. The normative order: incoming deadhead (null) -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after arrival, the UAV goes through turnaround period, occupying the pad? Usually turnaround time at the pad may use pad capacity. The pad capacity per slot is default 2, meaning max 2 aircraft can be at vertiport at any slot. So if both arrive at slot 2, and both undergo turnaround, they might exceed pad capacity? Pad capacity likely includes aircraft on the ground, including during turnaround. If both UAVs are at VP_02 at same time, that's 2, which equals capacity, so allowed. So depart 1 for MISSION_05 seems feasible.\n\nBut we need to check MISSION_02: if we use PATH_07 (COR_09) depart 0, travel 2, arrival slot 2. Then we need to do MISSION_03 from VP_02. MISSION_02 destination is VP_02, so after turnaround, UAV_01 is available at slot 3 (arrival 2 + turnaround 1). MISSION_03 release 9, due 18. So can depart later, e.g., depart 9? baseline depart 9. That's fine.\n\nNow energy for UAV_01: start 1.793999. MISSION_02 PATH_07 energy 0.23504, so after flight, SOC = 1.793999 - 0.23504 = 1.558959. Reserve 1.5, so SOC > 1.5, check pass. Then turnaround, then post-service charge. We could charge. For MISSION_03, path PATH_05 energy 0.104358. So we need at least 1.5+0.104358 = 1.604358 before MISSION_03. After charging, we can top up. Charge per block 1.2. Max blocks 3. So charging 1 block gives 1.2 kWh, bringing SOC to 1.558959 + 1.2 = 2.758959, after which we must check battery upper bound 16.0, fine. So we can set charge_blocks = 1 for MISSION_02 to ensure enough energy. But we also might want to minimize delay/charge wait? The objective includes \"delay plus charge wait\". So unnecessary charging might increase wait? Charge wait is the time spent charging? The post-service charge blocks occur after turnaround, occupying slots. Each charge block takes 1 slot? The problem doesn't specify slot duration for charging; charge_kwh_per_block is energy per block, and presumably each block takes 1 slot. So if we charge, the UAV is delayed from starting next mission. In baseline, no charging was used (all charge_blocks 0). So if we add charge blocks, it adds to departure slot of next mission. So we want to minimize charge blocks if possible without violating energy constraints. For UAV_01, we need to ensure enough for MISSION_03. SOC after MISSION_02 = 1.558959, reserve 1.5, so only 0.058959 above reserve. MISSION_03 requires 0.104358, which would bring SOC to 1.558959 - 0.104358 = 1.454601, which is below reserve 1.5. So we need charging. At least need to add enough energy so that after flight, SOC >= reserve. Required energy before MISSION_03: reserve + MISSION_03 energy = 1.604358. So we need at least 1.604358 - 1.558959 = 0.045399 kWh. Charge per block 1.2, so 1 block is plenty. So charge_blocks = 1. That adds 1 slot after turnaround. MISSION_02 arrival 2 (if depart 0), turnaround slot 2? Actually, arrival at slot 2, turnaround takes 1 slot (slot 2 to 3? Typically, if arrival at slot t, turnaround occupies slot t, and UAV becomes available at t+1. So arrival 2, turnaround during slot 2, available at 3. Post-service charge then occurs after turnaround at the destination. It says: \"post-service charge\" occurs after the stated turnaround. So the charge blocks start after turnaround. So if turnaround ends at slot 3, charging block 1 would occupy slot 3 (or slot 3 if we charge 1 block). Then UAV available at slot 4. So MISSION_03 could depart slot 4 or later. Release 9, so that's fine. So no additional delay beyond release. However, the baseline MISSION_03 depart 9, so we could keep depart 9. That means we could delay MISSION_03 departure to 9 even if we finish charging at 4, just wait. So charge_blocks 1 does not force a later departure than 9. So we can set charge_blocks=1 for MISSION_02.\n\nNow, MISSION_05: we might want to shift depart to 1 to free COR_09 slot 0. That changes depart_slot from 0 to 1, increasing repair distance. Could we instead keep MISSION_05 at 0 and use PATH_09 for MISSION_02? PATH_09 uses COR_02, which has capacity 2, no override, so no conflict. But PATH_09 travel slots 3, energy 0.2486. With depart_slot 0, arrival slot 3. Then SOC after MISSION_02 = 1.793999 - 0.2486 = 1.545399. Reserve 1.5, so SOC = 0.045399 above reserve. After that, need MISSION_03 energy 0.104358, insufficient without charge. Charge 1 block: need 1.604358 - 1.545399 = 0.058959, so 1 block enough. Turnaround arrival 3, turnaround slot 3, available 4. Charge 1 block at slot 4, available 5. MISSION_03 can depart 9, still fine. So using PATH_09 avoids the COR_09 conflict entirely, no need to shift MISSION_05. That would keep MISSION_05 depart 0, MISSION_02 path changes from PATH_04 to PATH_09, depart_slot same 0? Baseline MISSION_02 depart 0, path PATH_04. Changing path and maybe charge_blocks changes repair distance. Repair distance likely penalizes any difference in path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks, held. So path change from PATH_04 to PATH_09 adds a difference. But if we shift MISSION_05 depart 1, we change its depart_slot (0 to 1), plus MISSION_02 path change (to PATH_07 or PATH_09). So we need to compare number of changes. Baseline had MISSION_02 PATH_04 with charge_blocks 0, depart 0. MISSION_05 PATH_03 depart 0. Now with interventions, both MISSION_02 must change path because PATH_04 is infeasible due to low SOC; we must change path anyway. So change in MISSION_02 is unavoidable. MISSION_05 could remain depart 0 if we use PATH_09, but PATH_09 also changes path from PATH_04, so same. MISSION_05 depart 1 would add another change. So minimizing changes: keep MISSION_05 depart 0, and use PATH_09 for MISSION_02. Then MISSION_02 changes path and charge_blocks (0->1). That's 2 attribute changes perhaps. If we use PATH_07 and shift MISSION_05, that changes MISSION_05 depart (0->1) and MISSION_02 path and charge. So similar. But repair distance might be sum of absolute depart_slot differences; shifting MISSION_05 by 1 adds 1, plus any path changes maybe a constant penalty. Usually in these tasks, repair distance is the number of discrete changes: if decision changed, it counts. So better to keep MISSION_05 unchanged. So use PATH_09 for MISSION_02.\n\nNow MISSION_04: baseline depart 3 with PATH_08. Both path and slot infeasible due to closure. Need to change path to either PATH_02 or PATH_06, and likely later departure due to closures on PATH_02 and COR_07 closure 3-14, so must depart at 15 or later. PATH_06 (COR_01) not closed, but travel 14 slots, energy 1.337981. Could depart at 3? release 3. Depart 3, travel 14, arrive 17. Turnaround 1, available 18. That's before MISSION_01 release 19. So PATH_06 possible depart 3. Check COR_01 capacity: default 2, no closure. So PATH_06 feasible. Energy: after MISSION_05, UAV_02 SOC 4.611226. MISSION_04 PATH_06 energy 1.337981, so after flight SOC = 3.273245. Reserve 2.5, okay. Then MISSION_01 PATH_01 energy 1.25632, total after MISSION_04 SOC 3.273245 - 1.25632 = 2.016925, above reserve 2.5? Wait, 2.016925 < 2.5, that's below reserve. So need to check reserve after MISSION_01. Reserve check occurs after incoming deadhead + service flight (i.e., after flight arrival). So after MISSION_01, SOC must be >= reserve 2.5. With SOC 2.016925, violation. So we need charging between MISSION_04 and MISSION_01. We can charge after MISSION_04 at VP_04. After MISSION_04, SOC = 3.273245. Before MISSION_01, we need at least reserve 2.5 + MISSION_01 energy 1.25632 = 3.75632. So we need charging of at least 3.75632 - 3.273245 = 0.483075 kWh. 1 charge block gives 0.95 kWh, enough. So charge_blocks=1 after MISSION_04. That adds 1 slot. MISSION_04 arrival at depart 3 + travel 14 = 17. Turnaround slot 17, available 18. Charge 1 block at 18, available 19. MISSION_01 release 19, so can depart exactly at 19, same as baseline depart 19. So MISSION_01 depart unchanged. However, MISSION_04 now uses PATH_06, depart_slot 3, charge_blocks 1. That's changes from baseline: path change, depart_slot same (3) but baseline depart 3? Actually baseline MISSION_04 depart_slot 3 as well, so depart_slot unchanged, only path and charge_blocks changed. So repair distance might be path change + charge change. Could we instead use PATH_02 with later departure? PATH_02 energy 0.15704, travel 2 slots. If we depart after COR_07 closure (slots 3-14), earliest depart 15? COR_07 closed 3-14, so open from 15. Depart 15, arrive 17, turnaround 17, available 18. Then need energy for MISSION_01: after MISSION_05, SOC 4.611226. MISSION_04 PATH_02: 0.15704, SOC = 4.454186. MISSION_01 needs 1.25632, total SOC after would be 3.197866, still below reserve? 3.197866 > 2.5, actually 3.197866 > 2.5, so reserve check after MISSION_01 passes. So no charging needed. But departure 15 for MISSION_04 changes depart_slot to 15 from baseline 3, increasing distance. And path changes. So PATH_06 keeps depart_slot same, but adds charge and path change. Which is better? Repair distance likely penalizes slot differences more than path changes? Usually, the objective \"repair distance\" is defined as sum of absolute differences in depart_slot plus possibly other penalties. Without precise formula, we need to infer from typical usage. In many air mobility repair tasks, repair distance is the sum of absolute differences of departure times from baseline, plus a penalty for number of path changes. But lexicographic order: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance. So we want to minimize holds first, then total holds (all missions must be served, so holds 0 if possible; can we hold any mission? Holding a mission removes it from schedule, which may simplify constraints but adds hold. Since holds are minimized before repair distance, we want to avoid holds if possible. All missions appear feasible, so holds 0 should be achievable. So repair distance is next. To minimize repair distance, we need to underst